This paper introduces EVPropNet, a deep convolutional neural network that
automatically identifies drones in mid-air by locating propellers within
a video stream. The network's architecture is designed to process video
frames in real-time, making it suitable for detecting and tracking drones
in real-world applications. The mid-air detection of drones poses a complex
challenge, requiring a high degree of accuracy in propeller detection.
By leveraging deep learning techniques, EVPropNet can identify small
propellers within video streams, even when they are partially occluded.
The network's performance was evaluated using a custom drone dataset
captured under various environmental conditions, demonstrating its
ability to detect propellers and track drones in real-time. The
state-of-the-art performance of EVPropNet, with an average detection rate
of 0.95, 0.87, and 0.87 frames per second on single, dual, and
quadcopter drones, respectively, showcases its potential for real-time
mid-air drone detection and tracking. The network's open-source code is
available on GitHub. \textcolor{blue}{\url{https://github.com/abhishekskumar2006/Propeller.} } 
